# Messages of the errors owned by arc-geom. Identifiers are registered in
# crates/arc-geom/src/error.rs.

geom-input-not-finite = A coordinate, length, angle, factor or transform entry is not a finite number.
geom-segment-zero-length = The segment starts and ends at the same point.
geom-direction-zero = The direction has no length.
geom-radius-not-positive = The radius must be a positive number.
geom-sweep-invalid = The arc must sweep more than zero and at most one full turn.
geom-ellipse-invalid-axis = The ellipse needs a major axis with a positive length and a minor-to-major ratio above zero and at most one.
geom-polyline-too-few-vertices = A polyline needs at least two vertices.
geom-polyline-zero-length-segment = Two consecutive polyline vertices are at the same point.
geom-polyline-negative-width = A polyline width cannot be negative.
geom-bbox-invalid = The bounding box minimum lies above its maximum.
geom-transform-singular = The transform cannot be inverted.
geom-transform-not-similarity = A polyline with arcs or widths can only be moved, rotated, mirrored or scaled uniformly.
geom-precision-insufficient = The result cannot be computed precisely enough.
geom-decode-invalid = The stored geometry is damaged or too large.
geom-tolerance-invalid = A tolerance is negative, not a finite number, or zero where a positive value is needed.
geom-view-eye-at-target = The eye and the target of the view are at the same point.
geom-view-up-invalid = The up direction must not be zero or parallel to the viewing direction.
geom-view-fov-invalid = The field of view must be greater than zero and less than a half turn.
geom-view-scale-not-positive = The view height must be a positive number.
geom-view-clip-invalid = The near clip distance must be smaller than the far one, and positive for a perspective view.
geom-view-aspect-invalid = The aspect ratio must be a positive number.
geom-length-out-of-range = The length lies outside the curve.
geom-curve-unbounded = The operation needs a curve with two ends; rays and lines have no end.
geom-flatten-too-many-vertices = Flattening the curve needs more vertices than the limit allows.
geom-region-loop-empty = A loop needs at least one curve.
geom-region-loop-open = The curves of the loop do not join end to end.
geom-region-loop-zero-area = The loop encloses no area.
geom-region-empty = A region needs at least one outer loop.
geom-region-hole-outside = A hole lies outside its outer loop.
geom-triangulate-edges-cross = The flattened boundary of the region crosses or touches itself.
geom-triangulate-loop-collapsed = A loop of the region collapses when flattened at this tolerance.
geom-triangulate-nesting-invalid = A hole lies outside its outer loop, or a part overlaps another.
geom-mesh-invalid = The triangle mesh has an invalid vertex, a triangle index outside its vertices, or a triangle that is not counterclockwise.
geom-intersect-too-many-results = The curves have more intersections than the limit allows.
geom-nurbs-degree-invalid = The degree of the NURBS curve must be at least 1, at most the supported maximum, and less than the number of control points.
geom-nurbs-knots-invalid = The knot vector of the NURBS curve is not valid.
geom-nurbs-weights-invalid = The NURBS curve needs one positive weight per control point.
geom-nurbs-degenerate = All control points of the NURBS curve are at the same place.
geom-nurbs-too-many-control-points = The NURBS curve has more control points than the limit allows.
geom-nurbs-knot-not-removable = The knot cannot be removed without changing the curve by more than the tolerance.
geom-fit-points-invalid = The curve needs more points for its degree, and no two consecutive points may be equal.
geom-parameter-out-of-range = The parameter lies outside the range the operation accepts.
geom-curve-kind-unsupported = The operation does not support this kind of curve yet.
geom-edit-point-off-curve = The point does not lie on the curve.
geom-edit-no-intersection = The boundary curves do not meet the curve.
geom-edit-curve-closed = The operation needs an open curve.
geom-composite-empty = A composite curve needs at least one curve.
geom-composite-not-connected = The curves of the composite do not join end to end.
geom-offset-collapsed = The offset distance is too large for the curve; nothing of the offset remains.
geom-offset-degenerate = The curve has a point without a direction, so it cannot be offset.
geom-offset-join-invalid = The miter limit must be a number of at least 1.
geom-offset-too-many-pieces = The offset needs more pieces than the limit allows.
geom-index-too-many-points = The point index would hold more points than the limit allows.
geom-region-self-intersecting = A curve of the region crosses or touches itself.
geom-region-curves-intersect = Two curves of the region cross, touch or overlap.
geom-arrangement-too-large = The curves form more pieces or intersections than the limit allows.
geom-trace-not-enclosed = No closed boundary encloses the picked point.
geom-trace-on-boundary = The picked point lies on a boundary curve.
geom-corner-radius-invalid = The fillet radius is not a finite number of zero or more.
geom-corner-distance-invalid = A chamfer distance is not a finite number of zero or more, or the chamfer angle is not between 0 and 180 degrees.
geom-corner-pick-off-curve = A pick point lies off the curve it selects.
geom-corner-pick-no-solution = No corner solution keeps the pick points on the parts that remain.
geom-corner-radius-too-large = No fillet of this radius is tangent to both curves at the corner.
geom-corner-no-intersection = The two curves do not meet, so they form no corner.
geom-corner-parallel = The two lines are parallel, so they form no corner.
geom-corner-coincident = The two curves lie on the same line or circle.
geom-corner-curve-too-short = A curve is too short to keep a part after the corner is cut.
geom-corner-unsupported-pair = The corner construction is not defined for these curve kinds.
geom-corner-segment-invalid = The polyline segment index is outside the polyline or repeated.
geom-construct-coincident-points = Two points that define the construction coincide.
geom-construct-collinear-points = The three points lie on one line, so no circle passes through them.
geom-construct-radius-too-small = The radius is less than half the distance between the two points, so no arc of that radius joins them.
geom-construct-sides-invalid = A regular polygon needs at least three sides and at most the supported maximum.
geom-construct-degenerate = The inputs have infinitely many solutions, so no finite set of circles is returned.
geom-solid-limit-exceeded = A solid has more vertices, faces or face-loop entries than the limit allows.
geom-solid-topology-invalid = A face of a solid has a loop with fewer than three vertices, a repeated vertex, an index outside the vertex list, a hole that is not inside its outer loop, a zero area or an edge not longer than the linear tolerance, or a vertex belongs to no face.
geom-solid-not-planar = A face of a solid has a vertex farther than the linear tolerance from the plane of the face.
geom-solid-not-closed = The boundary of a solid is not closed: an edge of a face has no matching edge in the opposite direction.
geom-solid-non-manifold = An edge of a solid is used by more than two faces, or two parts of the solid meet along a line that lies inside a face.
geom-solid-orientation-inconsistent = Two faces of a solid that share an edge traverse it in the same direction, so their orientations are inconsistent.
geom-solid-volume-not-positive = The volume of a solid is not positive: its faces point inward or it is flat.
geom-solid-extrusion-invalid = An extrusion has a height that is zero or not finite, or the target plane is not parallel to the plane of the region.
geom-solid-clip-degenerate = The cutting plane meets the solid in a way that does not give a valid result, such as a section that touches itself at a vertex or a plane nearly parallel to a face it crosses.
geom-solid-opening-unsupported = The prism does not pass fully through two parallel faces of the solid, with its outline inside both faces and clear of every other face; only that case of subtraction is supported.
geom-solid-opening-splits = The opening would split the solid into more separate parts than it has. Allow splitting on the tool only when the opening is meant to cut the solid in two; otherwise the axis or the profile is wrong.
